  "response": "```python\ndef bank_rollback(balance, transactions):\n    \"\"\"\n    Applies a series of financial transactions to an account balance.\n    If at any point during transaction processing the balance goes negative,\n    it returns the original balance and False (failed). Otherwise, it returns\n    the final balance and True (successful).\n\n    Args:\n        balance (float): Initial account balance.\n        transactions (list of float/int): List of transaction amounts.\n\n    Returns:\n        tuple: (final_balance, success_flag)\n                - If all transactions complete without negative balance: (final_balance, True)\n                - If balance becomes negative at any point: (original_balance, False)\n    \"\"\"\n    current_balance = balance\n    \n    for amount in transactions:\n        if current_balance + amount < 0:\n            return (balance, False)\n        current_balance += amount\n    \n    return (current_balance, True)\n\n\n# Test examples:\nprint(bank_rollback(100, [50, -30, -200]))   # Expected: (100, False)\nprint(bank_rollback(100, [50, -30]))         # Expected: (120, True)\nprint(bank_rollback(50, [-10, 20, -50]))     # Expected: (50, False)\nprint(bank_rollback(-50, [-10]))             # Expected: (-50, False)\nprint(bank_rollback(0, [10, -5, 5]))         # Expected: (10, True)\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n- The function applies each transaction sequentially to the current balance.\n- If at any point the running balance becomes negative, it returns a tuple with the original balance and `False`.\n- If all transactions are processed successfully without going negative, it returns the final balance and `True`.",
